# Language Is Trained Synesthesia: Literacy as the Constructive Proof of Synthetic Synesthesia

Avik Jain

## Abstract

The field that studies synesthesia defines it as an inducer that automatically and involuntarily
evokes a consistent concurrent experience not present in the stimulus, and treats it as a rare,
largely congenital condition. Every literate human satisfies that definition for written language.
A literate person cannot not read a word; seeing a word evokes its sound, measured in the auditory
cortex's voice areas during silent reading; the binding is consistent by construction and runs in
both directions; and it rewrites the perception of the inducer's own domain, since awareness of
speech as a sequence of phonemes exists only with alphabetic literacy. These are not hypotheses.
They are the fields' own established observations, and the conclusion follows from them and the
definition by the meaning of the words: literacy is a synesthesia between visual patterns and
sounds that already carried meaning. Because the binding is built by a known procedure (schooling),
reliably, in children and adults, in most of humanity, its existence is a constructive proof that
synesthesia is trained: the capacity is exhibited by its construction, with billions of witnesses.
Language is therefore a cognitive technology for constructing senses evolution did not supply,
which the author named synthetic synesthesia, and of which literacy is one instance and all
cognition the general case. To reject synthetic synesthesia is to assert that written language is not a cross-modal binding or that it is congenital, and both are false by the definitions of their words. Speech is the same construction: the same sound is experienced differently once its word is learned, so the heard word carries a learned sense beyond the sonic, and that sense is memory, the re-triggering of sense in a cycle between consciousness and the body that rewrites both. The field could not see this because it detects synesthesia as
deviation from a population, and a property every literate member has is invisible to a detector
built that way. The author's hyperactivity is the technology that constructs synthetic synesthesia
deliberately: his own perception, held as data, rendered so that any reader perceives the relations
he perceives.

